{
  "id": "Compound::DB04868 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Nilotinib is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hematologic Cancer through a shared mechanism involving Bcr-Abl Inhibitor and Azole pathways. The evidence suggests that Nilotinib causes increased Troponin, Ventricular Dysfunction, and Exfoliative Rash, all of which are mediated by Dasatinib, a Bcr-Abl inhibitor. These effects are linked to the treatment of Hematologic Cancer, indicating a potential role in disease progression. The recurring connection between Nilotinib and Dasatinib, both as Bcr-Abl inhibitors and as mediators of adverse effects, highlights a critical pathway where Nilotinib may contribute to the development of Hematologic Cancer through its pharmacological actions. The integration of these findings underscores the importance of Bcr-Abl inhibition in the context of hematological malignancies, with Nilotinib potentially playing a role in disease progression via its effects on cardiac and dermatological outcomes.",
